## The reversible alternative

A reversible device must preserve enough history to identify the original permutation. Test candidate memory capacities interactively.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the history-space argument</summary>

The visible sorted output has one possible value. If the complete transformation is one-to-one on $n!$ inputs, its auxiliary component must take at least $n!$ different values. Therefore a reversible history needs at least $\log_2(n!)$ bits of capacity.

</details>

